WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:04.189
Our planning team is ready to improve the invitation.

2
00:00:04.856 --> 00:00:07.233
The goal is not just to send a message.

3
00:00:07.767 --> 00:00:10.478
It is for people to receive information they can

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.056
use, understand what they are being asked, and have

5
00:00:14.078 --> 00:00:15.678
a workable way to respond.

6
00:00:16.431 --> 00:00:20.587
Think about the whole exchange: the invitation, the material

7
00:00:20.609 --> 00:00:24.120
someone needs to review, the conversation, the way they

8
00:00:24.142 --> 00:00:27.542
can ask for help, and the follow-up. A change

9
00:00:27.564 --> 00:00:30.187
to one step may not solve a barrier in

10
00:00:30.209 --> 00:00:33.698
another. The person using the process can tell you

11
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.231
what is and is not working for them.

12
00:00:36.911 --> 00:00:40.556
This lesson is voluntary and individual. You may use

13
00:00:40.578 --> 00:00:42.933
the shared example or a general process from your

14
00:00:42.956 --> 00:00:46.400
work. Keep any notes free of names, case details,

15
00:00:46.756 --> 00:00:49.400
health information, and other confidential information.

16
00:00:50.744 --> 00:00:53.833
The team has prepared a written notice in English

17
00:00:53.856 --> 00:00:57.478
and is considering what other support to offer. Rather

18
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.211
than assigning a need from someone's name, background, age,

19
00:01:01.456 --> 00:01:04.856
disability, or the community brief you read, make the

20
00:01:04.878 --> 00:01:07.878
request process easy to find and ask the person

21
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.322
what works for this communication.

22
00:01:10.227 --> 00:01:14.027
An open question could be, “What language or communication

23
00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:17.182
format would help you take part?” Depending on the

24
00:01:17.204 --> 00:01:20.471
situation, another question might be, “Would you like an

25
00:01:20.493 --> 00:01:24.338
interpreter or another communication support, and how should we

26
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.604
arrange it?” Ask about a practical need; do not

27
00:01:27.627 --> 00:01:30.449
demand a personal history as the price of access.

28
00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:34.269
Listen to the answer and check that you understood

29
00:01:34.291 --> 00:01:37.691
it. If an arrangement is already documented in an

30
00:01:37.713 --> 00:01:41.358
approved process, use it appropriately so the person does

31
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.313
not have to repeat the same request unnecessarily. If

32
00:01:45.336 --> 00:01:48.936
you cannot arrange a requested support yourself, explain whom

33
00:01:48.958 --> 00:01:51.402
you will contact and when the person can expect

34
00:01:51.424 --> 00:01:52.024
an update.

35
00:01:53.056 --> 00:01:57.189
Language assistance and disability access can overlap, but they

36
00:01:57.211 --> 00:02:00.500
are not identical. Someone may ask for an interpreter,

37
00:02:00.811 --> 00:02:04.522
a translated document, an accessible electronic file, a different

38
00:02:04.544 --> 00:02:06.789
way to communicate in a meeting, or more time

39
00:02:06.811 --> 00:02:10.100
with a communication device. The right arrangement depends on

40
00:02:10.122 --> 00:02:12.544
the person, the information, and the situation.

41
00:02:13.448 --> 00:02:17.137
The National CLAS Standards call for language assistance at

42
00:02:17.159 --> 00:02:19.848
no cost to individuals in health and health care,

43
00:02:20.248 --> 00:02:24.314
notice of available assistance, and competent people providing it.

44
00:02:24.337 --> 00:02:28.159
The Department of Justice's effective-communication guidance describes aids and

45
00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:31.870
services for people with communication disabilities and the importance

46
00:02:31.892 --> 00:02:34.248
of the person's usual method and the context.

47
00:02:34.926 --> 00:02:38.659
Apply the relevant agency procedures and current requirements to

48
00:02:38.681 --> 00:02:41.503
your work; ask the responsible office when you are

49
00:02:41.526 --> 00:02:44.726
unsure. A short learning activity does not decide a

50
00:02:44.748 --> 00:02:46.592
legal obligation for every situation.

51
00:02:47.337 --> 00:02:50.092
Do not assume that a relative, a child, or

52
00:02:50.114 --> 00:02:54.070
a bilingual colleague should interpret. Follow the applicable process

53
00:02:54.092 --> 00:02:57.870
for qualified assistance and privacy. If a person invites

54
00:02:57.892 --> 00:03:00.514
a supporter, ask what role they want that person

55
00:03:00.537 --> 00:03:04.181
to have; a supporter's presence does not automatically replace

56
00:03:04.203 --> 00:03:05.737
the person's own participation.

57
00:03:07.061 --> 00:03:10.972
Return to the planning opportunity. A person tells the

58
00:03:10.994 --> 00:03:13.528
team that the current materials do not work with

59
00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:17.328
their communication method. Start by asking what would make

60
00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:21.594
the materials and conversation usable. Confirm what support is

61
00:03:21.617 --> 00:03:24.172
needed, who can arrange it, and when it will

62
00:03:24.194 --> 00:03:27.617
be ready. Check both the document and the meeting,

63
00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.083
not just one of them.

64
00:03:29.689 --> 00:03:32.844
Make the next step clear. The team might provide

65
00:03:32.867 --> 00:03:36.711
an accessible version, arrange a qualified service, offer a

66
00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:39.600
usable way to send a response, or consult the

67
00:03:39.622 --> 00:03:44.244
appropriate accessibility or language-access contact. Which steps fit depends

68
00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:47.867
on the actual request and applicable procedures. Do not

69
00:03:47.889 --> 00:03:50.733
promise an arrangement you have not confirmed; do not

70
00:03:50.756 --> 00:03:52.733
leave the person to solve the problem alone.

71
00:03:53.306 --> 00:03:57.106
Afterward, ask whether the arrangement worked and what remains

72
00:03:57.128 --> 00:04:00.483
difficult. Record only what is needed and allowed by

73
00:04:00.506 --> 00:04:03.883
the applicable process. A useful record helps the next

74
00:04:03.906 --> 00:04:06.550
step happen; it is not a collection of personal

75
00:04:06.572 --> 00:04:07.506
cultural stories.

76
00:04:08.612 --> 00:04:12.079
A participant says the projected captions at a planning

77
00:04:12.101 --> 00:04:15.812
meeting are not usable for them. The meeting organizer

78
00:04:15.834 --> 00:04:19.190
has not asked what alternative would work. What would

79
00:04:19.212 --> 00:04:19.946
you do next?

80
00:04:20.824 --> 00:04:24.247
Pause and choose an answer in the activity. Then

81
00:04:24.269 --> 00:04:27.758
compare the explanations. Consider what you know about the

82
00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.602
participant's request and what you would need to check

83
00:04:30.624 --> 00:04:31.647
before deciding.

84
00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:37.619
Choose an invitation, notice, meeting, or form you can

85
00:04:37.641 --> 00:04:40.908
influence, or continue with the example. Write down how

86
00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:44.663
someone can request language or communication support; what must

87
00:04:44.686 --> 00:04:47.552
happen before they participate; and who can help if

88
00:04:47.574 --> 00:04:50.552
you cannot resolve the request. Add one way to

89
00:04:50.574 --> 00:04:52.797
check whether the arrangement actually worked.

90
00:04:53.516 --> 00:04:57.560
Use the existing access checklist and CLAS learning when

91
00:04:57.582 --> 00:05:01.138
you need more detail, and confirm current agency procedures.

92
00:05:02.093 --> 00:05:04.560
Next we will focus on the person's voice and

93
00:05:04.582 --> 00:05:06.938
on the relationships they choose to include in a

94
00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:07.404
decision.
